---

<SummerLayout title="Connections to the Novel — Things Fall Apart" section="tfa" spoke="novel">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SPOKE HEADER — umber band + UliSun watermark + Akwete chevron
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="tfa-spoke-header">
    <div class="tfa-spoke-header-watermark" aria-hidden="true">
      <UliSun size={440} line="var(--t-cream)" />
    </div>
    <div class="tfa-spoke-header-inner">
      <span class="tfa-spoke-header-eyebrow">Things Fall Apart · Spoke 05</span>
      <div class="tfa-spoke-badge-row">
        <div class="tfa-spoke-badge" style="border-color: var(--t-forest);"><TSpokeGlyph idx={4} size={38} color="var(--t-cream)" /></div>
        <h1 class="tfa-spoke-header-title">Connections to the Novel</h1>
      </div>
      <p class="tfa-spoke-header-deck">An epic beginning, and the tragic descent it names. What Achebe builds before it breaks, what Okonkwo carries, how the church worked through the culture's own fault lines — and the reasonable paragraph the whole novel was written to answer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="tfa-header-chevron" aria-hidden="true">
    <AkweteChevron width={1200} height={9} />
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SPOILER BANNER
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="tfa-spoiler" data-screen-label="Spoiler banner">
    <div class="tfa-spoiler-inner">
      <span class="tfa-spoiler-tag">Before you read on</span>
      <p class="tfa-spoiler-text">This section discusses major plot events, including the endings of several characters' storylines. If you are still reading the novel, you may want to return to this page after you finish <em>Things Fall Apart</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OPENING ORIENTATION — cream · no zone opener
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone" data-ground="cream" data-screen-label="Opening orientation">
    <div class="tfa-orient">
      <span class="tfa-orient-eyebrow">Spoke Five · Connections to the Novel</span>
      <p>The other pages on this site have covered the history and culture that <em>Things Fall Apart</em> inhabits: Igbo political structure and cosmology, the agricultural rhythms of Umuofia, British colonialism and its administrative machinery, the missionaries and the social disruptions they produced. This page builds the connections between that context and the specific cultural claims Achebe makes with his novel.</p>
      <div class="tfa-orient-rule" aria-hidden="true">
        <UliCurve width={120} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="cream" groundTo="cream" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ZONE 1 — An Epic Beginning · ground · still · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§1 An Epic Beginning">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <SectionOpener text="tfa" eyebrow="The Epic Frame" heading="An Epic Beginning" background="cream" motifIdx={4} />

      <div class="tfa-invoke" data-screen-label="§1 The Invocations">
        <span class="tfa-invoke-eyebrow">The Epic Invocation</span>
        <div class="tfa-invoke-grid">

          <figure class="tfa-invoke-col tfa-invoke-col--iliad">
            <img class="tfa-invoke-cover" src="/assets/tfa/iliad-cover.jpg" width="326" height="500" alt="Cover of The Iliad by Homer — a terracotta Greek helmet on a cream ground." loading="lazy" />
            <div class="tfa-invoke-id">
              <span class="tfa-invoke-word">anger</span>
              <span class="tfa-invoke-gloss"><em>mēnis</em> — the rage of Achilles</span>
            </div>
            <blockquote class="tfa-invoke-verse">
              <p><span class="tfa-invoke-first">Rage</span>—Goddess, sing the rage of Peleus' son Achilles,<br />
              murderous, doomed, that cost the Achaeans countless losses,<br />
              hurling down to the House of Death so many sturdy souls,<br />
              great fighters' souls, but made their bodies carrion,<br />
              feasts for the dogs and birds,<br />
              and the will of Zeus was moving toward its end.</p>
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption class="tfa-invoke-cap">
              <span class="tfa-invoke-tag">A story about superhuman anger and its consequences</span>
              <span class="tfa-invoke-tr">Trans. Robert Fagles</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <figure class="tfa-invoke-col tfa-invoke-col--odyssey">
            <img class="tfa-invoke-cover" src="/assets/tfa/odyssey-cover.webp" width="416" height="640" alt="Cover of The Odyssey by Homer — a black disc with a Greek-key border and a single red-irised eye, on a sky-blue ground with stylized waves." loading="lazy" />
            <div class="tfa-invoke-id">
              <span class="tfa-invoke-word">man</span>
              <span class="tfa-invoke-gloss"><em>andra</em> — Odysseus as the man</span>
            </div>
            <blockquote class="tfa-invoke-verse">
              <p>Sing in me, Muse, and through me tell the story<br />
              of that <span class="tfa-invoke-first">man</span> skilled in all ways of contending,<br />
              the wanderer, harried for years on end,<br />
              after he plundered the stronghold<br />
              on the proud height of Troy.</p>
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption class="tfa-invoke-cap">
              <span class="tfa-invoke-tag">A story about a great man — what it means to be a man?</span>
              <span class="tfa-invoke-tr">Trans. Robert Fitzgerald</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body tfa-passage-body--lede">
          <p>In the ancient Greek tradition initiated by Homer, each poem announces its subject in its opening breath. The <em>Iliad</em> begins with the word "anger" — <em>mēnis</em>, a word normally associated with the anger of the gods that describes the famous rage of Achilles. The <em>Odyssey</em> begins with the word "man" — <em>andra</em>, a word that signifies something like a "man's man," used to describe Odysseus as the man "skilled in all ways of contending." That first word is a promise about the subject of the entire story. The hero who carries the burden of that word represents the values of an entire civilization, written larger than life. The Homeric epics celebrate Greek culture, but they also expose the cost, contradictions, and limitations of Greek cultural values.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="sr-pullquote sr-pullquote--tfa tfa-zone-pull">
        <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--top" aria-hidden="true"><UliTendril width={80} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} /></div>
        <blockquote class="sr-pullquote-body"><p>"It was this man that Okonkwo threw in a fight which the old men agreed was one of the fiercest since the founder of their town engaged a spirit of the wild for seven days and seven nights."</p></blockquote>
        <figcaption class="sr-pullquote-attribution">— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe (opening pages)</figcaption>
        <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--bottom" aria-hidden="true"><UliTendril width={80} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} /></div>
      </figure>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>Achebe opens <em>Things Fall Apart</em> with a similar focus on the defining characteristics of one man. Okonkwo's wrestling victory is measured not against other men but against a founding myth — "the founder of their town engaged a spirit of the wild for seven days and seven nights." His fame has "grown like a bush-fire in the harmattan." He is "tall and huge"; he "seem[s] to walk on springs, as if he [is] going to pounce on somebody." This is a hero introduced the way epic introduces heroes: as the outsized embodiment of his community's ideals.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>If Achebe seems to present his main character as a culture-defining epic hero, he also echoes the Homeric tradition of cultural critique. On the first page, the word Achebe associates with his hero is fame — the public reputation that Okonkwo has gained through achievement and feats of strength. But there is another even more potent word that comes to define the experience of Okonkwo.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="sr-pullquote sr-pullquote--tfa tfa-zone-pull">
        <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--top" aria-hidden="true"><UliTendril width={80} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} /></div>
        <blockquote class="sr-pullquote-body"><p>"Perhaps down in his heart Okonkwo was not a cruel man. But his whole life was dominated by fear, the fear of failure and of weakness. It was deeper and more intimate than the fear of evil and capricious gods and of magic, the fear of the forest, and of the forces of nature, malevolent, red in tooth and claw. Okonkwo's fear was greater than these. It was not external but lay deep within himself. It was the fear of himself, lest he should be found to resemble his father."</p></blockquote>
        <figcaption class="sr-pullquote-attribution">— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe (Chapter 2)</figcaption>
        <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--bottom" aria-hidden="true"><UliTendril width={80} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} /></div>
      </figure>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>Just as the plot of the <em>Iliad</em> is entirely defined by the anger of Achilles and its horrifying consequences, the fear of Okonkwo is the driving force of the story of <em>Things Fall Apart</em>. Fear of failure and weakness, fear of comparison to his embarrassing father, fear of insufficient manhood, all combine to produce a paralyzing anxiety in Okonkwo that expresses itself in self-defeating acts of cruelty and violence.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-subsection tfa-z1-herakles" data-screen-label="§1 The Unseasonal Hero">
        <div class="tfa-subsection-head">
          <span class="tfa-subsection-eyebrow">Nagy · Herakles · kleos</span>
          <h3 class="tfa-subsection-title">The Unseasonal Hero</h3>
        </div>
        <figure class="tfa-herakles-fig">
          <img src="/assets/tfa/herakles-unseasonal-hero.png" width="1376" height="768" alt="A line illustration in an antique sepia palette. At left, the goddess Hera stands on a cloud-wreathed rock, reaching out a peacock feather. At right, a muscular hero in a lion's-skin cloak leans hard into a vast field of flowing, swirling lines that stream toward him from Hera, bracing himself against the current as he strides forward." loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption class="tfa-herakles-cap"><strong>Hera and Herakles.</strong> In the reading of classicist Gregory Nagy, the hero's name means "the glory (<em>kleos</em>) of Hera" — the goddess who persecutes him is also the source of his greatness. <span class="tfa-herakles-credit">Original illustration created for this site.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>The classicist Gregory Nagy famously defines the ancient Greek conception of the hero as a person who is fundamentally unseasonal — "out of season," born at the wrong time, temporally displaced. The story of Herakles is the defining case that shows that this temporal displacement is the ironic engine of the Greek hero's greatness. The goddess Hera delays his birth so that he is born after, and subordinate to, an inferior cousin, a disadvantage that forces him into the famous Labors that earn his glory. Nagy reads his name itself as the story in compressed form — Hēra + kleos, "the glory that comes from Hera." Kleos is literally the words said about a hero after he is dead, the mortal life that becomes imperishable song. Achilles earns his kleos with an untimely death before the end of the Trojan war; Odysseus is displaced in time and space by the wanderings that keep him away from home for twenty years. According to Nagy, it is exactly the friction created by alienation, displacement, being the wrong person or in the wrong place, that pushes these heroes to the great deeds that lead to a memorable life and generate kleos.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <figure class="tfa-descent" data-screen-label="§1 The Descent" aria-label="A descending staircase of three platforms. Top left: Okonkwo's full flame-emblem in terracotta, in season, standing on a yam-barn ground. A step down, labeled exile, to a cooled mid-tone emblem standing on a bed of glowing, flickering coals — the fire nearly out but not dead. A step down, labeled the mission, marked with a cross, into a cold haze where the figure is reduced to a bare umber mark beside the closed Commissioner's book, the rising song struck through.">
        <span class="tfa-descent-eyebrow">In season → displaced → out of season</span>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 720 392" role="img" aria-hidden="true">
          <defs>
            <radialGradient id="tfa-ember-bed" cx="50%" cy="50%" r="50%">
