        </svg>
        <p class="tfa-descent-cap">Okonkwo begins as the embodiment of his world, the roaring flame that is the literal meaning of his name. Exile uproots him to borrowed ground where his fire begins to flicker. Then Christianity and British authority arrive in a dousing wave to quench his cultural authority forever, reducing an epic life to a paragraph in another man's book.</p>
      </figure>

      <div class="tfa-passage">
        <div class="tfa-passage-body">
          <p>Okonkwo's story sits in uneasy tension with this Greek conception of heroism. When we meet him, he is the ideal man of Umuofia at its height, perfectly in tune with the world that made him. Over the course of the novel, history and contingency transform him into an unseasonal man. The disastrous explosion of Okonkwo's gun at Ogbuefi Ezeudu's funeral displaces him into seven years of exile. His friend Obierika (another man who seemingly occupies a central cultural position in Umuofia) reflects on the justice of this punishment in one of the most complex moments of cultural representation in the entire novel.</p>

          <figure class="sr-pullquote sr-pullquote--tfa tfa-zone-pull">
            <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--top" aria-hidden="true"><UliTendril width={80} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} /></div>
            <blockquote class="sr-pullquote-body"><p>"Obierika was a man who thought about things. When the will of the goddess had been done, he sat down in his obi and mourned his friend's calamity. Why should a man suffer so grievously for an offense he had committed inadvertently? But although he thought for a long time he found no answer. He was merely led into greater complexities. He remembered his wife's twin children, whom he had thrown away. What crime had they committed? The Earth had decreed that they were an offense on the land and must be destroyed. And if the clan did not exact punishment for an offense against the great goddess, her wrath was loosed on all the land and not just on the offender. As the elders said, if one finger brought oil it soiled the others."</p></blockquote>
            <figcaption class="sr-pullquote-attribution">— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe (final paragraph of Part One)</figcaption>
            <div class="sr-pullquote-bracket sr-pullquote-bracket--bottom" aria-hidden="true"><UliTendril width={80} color="var(--t-terra)" opacity={0.7} /></div>
          </figure>

          <figure class="tfa-obierika" data-screen-label="§1 Obierika weighs it" aria-label="Obierika sits mourning in his obi at left, his bowed head wreathed in warm uli tendrils carrying his unanswerable questions. A sweeping line carries those questions rightward onto the left pan of a balance scale resting on Ala, the earth goddess. That pan holds the blameless and rides high; the right pan holds a heavy umber mass, all the land, and drags the beam down.">
            <span class="tfa-obierika-eyebrow">Anguish weighed against imperative</span>
