            <figcaption class="tfa-obierika-cap">Obierika mourns, and cannot answer his own question. His grief is set on the scale of Ala. The offended earth must be appeased, or her wrath falls on everyone.</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>Obierika's reflection captures the arbitrary nature of his great friend's punishment, but it also presents both Okonkwo's exile and his own abandonment of his twin children as necessary evils that preserve the fabric of society. When Okonkwo returns to Umuofia, he finds that the colonial encounter has transformed his society into a place where his old authority can find no purchase. The definition of the social good has changed so radically that his attempt to start a violent rebellion against British authority leads to "tumult instead of action." The realization that no one will respond to his call for resistance seems to leave Okonkwo with no other choice but to violate the powerful Igbo cultural taboo against suicide. Obierika is forced to pay the British officials to dispose of his friend's body because "they cannot bury him... Only strangers can." Here we see the ultimate inversion of Nagy's heroic formula. Herakles' unseasonality makes him a hero and earns him immortal <em>kleos</em>; Okonkwo's unmakes him and diminishes his story to a paragraph in the book of a British "stranger."</p>
        </div>
